Based on my analysis, here's how to use the
detect_consensus
function:

Function Signature
def detect_consensus(
    harvested_data: List[Dict], 
    match_criteria: Dict = None,
    confidence_threshold: float = 0.7
) -> List[CandidateEntity]
Purpose
This function performs cross-source verification by identifying consensus among multiple data sources about the same entities (people, organizations, locations). It compares facts from different sources and marks them as verified when they match across sources.

Parameters
Parameter	Type	Description	Default
harvested_data
List[Dict]
List of raw data dictionaries from the HARVESTING stage, each containing source information and facts	Required
match_criteria
Dict
Optional criteria for matching entities (e.g., which fields to compare)	
None
confidence_threshold
float
Minimum consensus level required for verification (0.0-1.0)	
0.7
Return Value
Returns a list of
CandidateEntity
objects, each containing:

entity_id: Unique identifier
name: Entity name
verified_score: Confidence score based on consensus
sources_list: List of source names that confirmed this entity
consensus_level: Agreed-upon confidence level
Usage Example
from osint_analyst_stage import detect_consensus, CandidateEntity

# Sample harvested data from multiple sources
harvested_data = [
    {
        "source": "social_media",
        "facts": [
            {"type": "name", "value": "John Smith"},
            {"type": "email", "value": "john@example.com"}
        ]
    },
    {
        "source": "public_records", 
        "facts": [
            {"type": "name", "value": "John Smith"},
            {"type": "email", "value": "john.smith@email.com"}
        ]
    },
    {
        "source": "leak_database",
        "facts": [
            {"type": "name", "value": "John Smith"},
            {"type": "phone", "value": "+1-555-0123"}
        ]
    }
]

# Detect consensus with default settings (70% confidence threshold)
verified_entities = detect_consensus(harvested_data)

for entity in verified_entities:
    print(f"Verified: {entity.name} ({entity.verified_score:.1%})")
    print(f"  Sources: {', '.join(entity.sources_list)}")
Advanced Usage
Custom Match Criteria
match_criteria = {
    "name_fields": ["full_name", "alias"],
    "location_fields": ["address", "city", "country"],
    "contact_fields": ["email", "phone"]
}

verified_entities = detect_consensus(
    harvested_data, 
    match_criteria=match_criteria,
    confidence_threshold=0.9  # Require 90% consensus
)
With Privacy Mode
from osint_analyst_stage import detect_consensus, analyze_osint_data

# Use within the main analyst workflow
analysis_report = analyze_osint_data(
    harvest_output, 
    privacy_mode='private'  # Anonymizes PII before analysis
)

verified_entities = analysis_report.verified_results
Integration with Pipeline
The function is typically called as part of the ANALYST stage after HARVESTING:

graph LR
    A[HARVEST] --> B[ANALYST - detect_consensus]
    B --> C{Consensus > Threshold?}
    C -->|Yes| D[Mark Verified]
    C -->|No| E[Flag for Manual Review]
    D --> F[Generate Report]
Key Considerations
Threshold Tuning: Lower thresholds (0.5-0.6) catch more entities but increase false positives; higher thresholds (0.8-0.9) are stricter but may miss valid matches
Privacy Mode: When using
privacy_mode='private'
, PII is anonymized before consensus detection to protect sensitive data
Performance: For large datasets (>10,000 entities), consider batching the analysis or adjusting batch size parameters
